blueprint metalcraft-agent
  title The Pod
  tagline >
    one rust binary that runs an agent as a scoped persona, on a box you own
  branch feat/pod-drive-share-and-polish
  stamp v0.29.0 · edition 2024

stat Rust = 28,794 lines · 91 files
stat Binaries = 2 — cli · daemon
stat Tools = 54 built-in + 30 app
stat HTTP routes = 59 on the workshop API
stat Seeded = 8 personas · 16 skills
stat Turn ceiling = 90 steps

group ways_in "Ways in"
  note four doors, one turn primitive

entry cli_repl "CLI" x1.2
  is the rustyline REPL, one-shot task mode, and the slash commands
  why >
    Parses the invocation, creates a diagnostics session, and either runs a
    single task or loops on a prompt. Slash commands that change persona,
    model, or working directory rebuild the whole agent, because all three are
    baked into the system prompt. If stdin is not a TTY it forces auto-approve
    and demands a one-shot task, so piping into it can never block on a hidden
    prompt.
  src src/main.rs src/cli.rs
  uses rustyline crossterm atty
  num Slash commands = 6

entry daemon_bin "Daemon"
  is >
    the scheduler process — polls flows, fires follow-ups, optionally hosts
    the API
  why >
    A thin binary that delegates straight into daemon::run. Beyond the flow
    poll it spawns three long-lived side loops: the workshop server when a key
    is present, the gateway self-heal loop, and the calendar reminder ticker.
    One process can therefore be the whole product.
  src src/bin/metalcraft-daemon.rs src/daemon.rs
  uses tokio
  num Default poll = 30 s

svc workshop_api "Workshop API" x1.9
  is >
    an Axum REST surface over every file the agent reads, plus live chat over
    SSE
  why >
    By far the largest file in the repo at 4,344 lines and 59 routes. It reads
    and writes the same data-dir files the CLI and daemon use, so an edit made
    through the API takes effect on the next agent build with no reload step.
    /health and /info are open; everything else needs a bearer that is either
    the static workshop key or a Metalcraft ID token verified against the hub.
  src src/workshop_api.rs src/hub_auth.rs openapi/workshop-api.yaml
  uses axum utoipa tokio-stream
  num Routes = 59
  num Lines = 4,344

svc gateway_ingress "Inbound gateway"
  is webhook push and long-poll pull, funnelled through one idempotency check
  why >
    Messages arrive either as a signed POST to /webhook/gateway or by holding
    a 25-second long-poll against the gateway's inbound/next. Both paths
    converge on route_gateway_inbound, which dedupes on the gateway message
    UUID before running a turn — so the gateway's dual-delivery mode cannot
    make the agent answer twice. The dedup ledger lives on the pod disk, so a
    redelivery after a restart is still caught.
  src src/inbound_dedup.rs src/workshop_api.rs
  uses hmac sha256

svc channels "Channels"
  is named outbound connections to a messaging gateway, resolved by slug
  why >
    A channel is just { slug, name, url, secret }. There is always a default
    one pointing at the first-party gateway and authenticated with the pod
    token the control plane injects, so the common case needs no configuration
    at all. The connect call fetches the base URL, integration id and webhook
    secret and registers the pod's inbound webhook in one shot, rather than
    asking a human to paste anything.
  src src/channels.rs src/metalcraft_gateway.rs
  uses reqwest

group the_turn "The turn"
  note compact, then run the ReAct loop

svc agent_runtime "Agent runtime" x1.3
  is >
    assembles persona prompt, tool registry, model and hooks into one compiled
    graph
  why >
    Renders the persona's mustache-style system prompt, resolves its tool
    names into a registry, builds the OpenAI-compatible model, attaches the
    approval and LLM hooks, and compiles a ReAct graph. The graph is stored
    behind an Arc so it can be cloned across turns for free. Any change to
    persona, model or cwd means calling this again — there is no partial
    rebuild.
  src src/runtime.rs
  uses metalcraft rig

svc turn_runner "Turn runner" x1.6
  is the single per-turn primitive: compact the context, then run the executor
  why >
    This exists because there are three ways to start a turn — the REPL, a
    workshop or gateway chat, and a one-shot flow task — and a behaviour
    present in one path used to go silently missing from the others. Folding
    compaction, the 90-step ceiling and the executor wiring into one place is
    what stops that. The step guard is passed in per call rather than held,
    because its lifetime is session-long in the CLI and per-turn in the
    daemon.
  src src/runtime.rs
  num Callers = 3 paths

ext react_graph "ReAct graph" x1.2
  is the think → call tool → observe loop, provided by the metalcraft crate
  why >
    Not code in this repo: the agent graph, executor, tool trait and hook
    system all come from the metalcraft framework crate. This repo supplies
    the system prompt, the registry and the hooks, and the framework drives
    the loop until a final answer or the step ceiling. Treating the loop as a
    dependency is what keeps the interesting logic here in hooks rather than
    in a bespoke agent implementation.
  src src/runtime.rs
  uses metalcraft rig
  num Max steps = 90

model llm_client "Model" x1.3
  is an OpenAI-compatible completion model, gpt-5.4 unless overridden
  why >
    Built from a single required environment variable and swapped at runtime
    by the /model slash command. A second, lighter instance is created
    separately as the compaction summarizer, so a long conversation is not
    summarized by the expensive model. Only the API key is mandatory to run
    the whole product.
  uses rig openai
  num Default = gpt-5.4

svc approval_hook "Approval" x1.2
  is >
    classifies every tool call into an operation kind, then auto-approves or
    prompts
  why >
    The policy is data, not conditionals: each call maps to an OperationKind
    which maps to a permission level. Writing a new file is automatic,
    overwriting an existing one prompts. Crucially, an unrecognised tool
    classifies as Execute and therefore prompts — it fails safe. Approvals are
    remembered per session and per path, and the terminal prompt runs on its
    own OS thread so it never fights rustyline for the terminal or blocks the
    tokio runtime.
  src src/approval.rs src/diff_preview.rs
  num Operation kinds = 11

svc step_guard "Step guard"
  is watches for error spirals and loops, and prints every call as it happens
  why >
    Stops the run after three consecutive all-error tool turns, or after the
    same tool is called byte-for-byte identically five times in a row. Tools
    marked as status polls are exempt and get a budget of sixty instead, so
    polling an async job is not mistaken for a runaway. Calls the user denied
    are retracted from both tallies, so rejecting something cannot itself trip
    the guard.
  src src/guard.rs
  num Error spiral = 3
  num Repeat limit = 4
  num Poll budget = 60

svc compactor "Compaction"
  is >
    summarizes old history once the estimated context passes 60% of the window
  why >
    Token counting is a deliberately cheap heuristic — roughly four characters
    per token across all message content — rather than a real tokenizer.
    Everything but the ten most recent messages is rendered into a transcript,
    summarized by a lightweight model, and replaced with one assistant
    message. The split point walks backwards to avoid severing a tool call
    from its result.
  src src/context.rs
  num Window = 128k
  num Trigger = 60%
  num Keep = 10 msgs

store diagnostics "Diagnostics & traces"
  is >
    per-session JSON snapshots plus an OTLP document following the GenAI
    conventions
  why >
    Two sibling writers sharing one session id, so the directories line up one
    to one. sessions/ holds bespoke per-turn message dumps and config-change
    records; traces/ holds a single OpenTelemetry JSON document you can ship
    to any collector. The workshop API can read both back, which is how you
    debug a run that happened while nobody was watching.
  src src/diagnostics.rs src/trace.rs src/diagnostics_browse.rs
  uses opentelemetry

group toolbox "The toolbox"

svc tool_registry "Tool registry" x1.5
  is >
    resolves a persona's tool names, with a four-stage fallthrough for unknown
    ones
  why >
    A persona lists tools by name and the registry matches them. What makes it
    extensible is what happens on a miss: the name is offered to the native
    pack tools, then to the pod apps, and finally to a JSON file in
    api_tools/. That is why adding a GitHub or Linear tool needs no Rust at
    all. The persona's list is the security boundary — nothing outside it is
    ever registered.
  src src/tools/mod.rs
  num Built-in tools = 54

lib core_tools "Core & delivery" x1.2
  is >
    file, shell, search and fetch tools, plus the three that talk back to the
    user
  why >
    read_file, write_file, edit_file, bash, list_files, grep, find_files,
    web_fetch and load_skill are the hand-written Rust ones. Alongside them
    sit say_to_user, gateway_send_message and schedule_followup — the delivery
    tools, which are what make a tool-only session possible: the agent has no
    stdout, so replying is itself a tool call.
  src src/tools/
  uses htmd reqwest

svc http_api_tool "HTTP API tool" x1.2
  is >
    one generic Rust tool that becomes any REST call, configured entirely in
    JSON
  why >
    A file in api_tools/ supplies the name, description, method, URL, headers,
    parameter schema and body mapping; at call time the tool expands path
    placeholders from the arguments, drops unfilled optional query segments,
    resolves $ENV_VAR references against the key store, and sends the request.
    This is how the GitHub, Linear, Cloudflare and Sentry packs work without a
    line of Rust between them.
  src src/tools/http_api.rs
  num Timeout = 30 s
  num Body truncation = 50k chars

lib meta_tools "Meta tools"
  is the agent editing its own personas, skills, flows, packs and keys
  why >
    persona_*, skill_*, flow_*, pack_*, key_* and diagnostics_* let a running
    agent rewrite its own configuration — which is exactly why the approval
    policy splits them: reads are automatic, writes prompt. The config-agent
    persona exists to use these and little else, so the reflexive capability
    is scoped rather than ambient.
  src src/tools/meta_persona.rs src/tools/meta_flow.rs src/tools/meta_skill.rs src/tools/meta_keys.rs

svc sub_agent_tool "Sub-agent"
  is spawns a nested agent with its own registry, on a 120-second leash
  why >
    Takes a task and a tool set — read-only by default, optionally scoped to
    one pack or to a named persona — then builds a fresh registry and graph
    and runs them. It returns the child's answer plus which tools it used and
    how many turns it took, so the parent can see the cost. This is the
    mechanism behind the default Orchestrator persona, which delegates rather
    than doing.
  src src/tools/sub_agent.rs
  num Timeout = 120 s
  num Max steps = 90

lib native_integrations "Native integrations"
  is the integrations that needed real Rust — S3, IMAP mail, Twilio
  why >
    Everything that fits a plain REST call lives in JSON instead. These three
    did not: S3 needs request signing, mail needs an IMAP session and MIME
    parsing, Twilio needs its own signature scheme. They are registered by
    pack id rather than individually, so enabling the email pack brings its
    whole tool family at once.
  src src/tools/s3.rs src/tools/email_imap.rs src/tools/twilio.rs
  uses imap mail-parser native-tls

group extending "What you add"
  note everything here is a file on disk

svc seeder "Seeder"
  is >
    writes the bundled personas, skills, packs and flows into the data dir on
    startup
  why >
    Every default is compiled into the binary with include_str! and written
    out on first run, so a fresh container is immediately useful with no fetch
    step. It will not clobber a file you have edited — with one exception: a
    bundled persona carrying a newer version number force-upgrades its
    installed copy, which is how fixes reach existing pods.
  src src/seed.rs seed/
  uses include_dir
  num Bundled = 8 personas · 16 skills

store persona_store "Personas" x1.4
  is JSON files defining name, system prompt, allowed tools and skills
  why >
    The central idea of the whole project: a request runs as a persona, which
    is a scoped tool set rather than a blank cheque. The research persona is
    read-only, the devops persona can touch its own infrastructure and nothing
    else. The prompt is a template — placeholders for cwd, available skills,
    sub-agent personas and installed packs are substituted, and any the author
    left out are appended as fallback sections.
  src src/persona.rs seed/personas/
  num Seeded = 8

store skill_store "Skills"
  is >
    markdown methodologies a persona loads on demand rather than carrying
    always
  why >
    Each is a file with YAML frontmatter and a body. The persona's skill list
    becomes an enum in load_skill's parameter schema, so the model can only
    ask for skills it is actually allowed to have. Keeping them out of the
    system prompt until requested is what stops sixteen methodologies from
    eating the context window before the first turn.
  src src/skill.rs seed/skills/
  num Seeded = 16

svc integration_packs "Integration packs" x1.3
  is >
    a zip of personas, skills and api_tools that installs as one unit and
    toggles on and off
  why >
    A pack manifest carries an id, version and required environment variables;
    installing one unpacks its parts into the same directories a human would
    edit. Enable-state is what gates its tools into the registry, and it gates
    the pod apps too — an app's id matches its pack id on purpose. Every
    install is also written to metalcraft.lock with a content hash, so a
    rebuilt pod can replay exactly the same versions instead of drifting to
    whatever is newest.
  src src/integration_packs.rs src/lockfile.rs seed/integration_packs/
  uses zip
  num Seeded packs = 8

store key_store "Key store"
  is >
    named secrets resolved from $NAME placeholders at call time, never at load
    time
  why >
    HTTP tool configs reference credentials as $GITHUB_TOKEN rather than
    embedding them, and the substitution happens when the request is built.
    That means a tool definition is safe to read, share, or hand to the model,
    and rotating a key takes effect on the next call with no reinstall. The
    API can list keys masked and reveal one explicitly.
  src src/key_store.rs

ext registries "Registries"
  is the public pack and flow registries the pod installs from
  why >
    Packs arrive as a zip from packs.metalcraftai.com and flows as a single
    validated JSON document from flows.metalcraftai.com. Both endpoints are
    public, so no credential is involved in installing. Downloads are
    size-capped before extraction, and both base URLs are overridable for
    self-hosting.
  src src/registry.rs src/flow_install.rs

group daemon "The daemon"

svc poll_loop "Poll loop" x1.3
  is >
    every thirty seconds: which flows are due, and which follow-ups have come
    round
  why >
    Loads the enabled flows, asks is_due for each against its schedule and
    timezone, marks the due ones running and executes them. Cron expressions
    use the six-field form with seconds and are evaluated in the daemon's
    local timezone, which is the detail that surprises people — set TZ=UTC if
    you want UTC. Interval run-state is in memory only, so a restart makes
    interval flows fire again immediately.
  src src/daemon.rs
  uses cron chrono-tz
  num Default tick = 30 s

store flow_store "Flows"
  is JSON graphs of nodes and edges, one file each, validated at load
  why >
    A flow declares a single entry node carrying the schedule, then a graph of
    prompt, tool, http, conditional and branch nodes. Validation runs at load
    time and a bad cron expression simply skips the flow rather than crashing
    the daemon. Schedule type manual means it is parsed and never auto-run —
    you trigger it from the API.
  src src/flows.rs seed/flow_templates/
  uses metalcraft-flows
  num Node types = 9

svc flow_exec_v2 "Flow state machine" x1.5
  is >
    walks the graph one node at a time, threading variables and routing by
    output handle
  why >
    The second-largest file in the repo. It is a real state machine rather
    than a script: approval and wait nodes park the run to disk and a later
    resume picks it up mid-graph, possibly days afterwards. A legacy v1 path
    still exists for older flows and just breadth-first collects reachable
    prompt nodes and runs each as a one-shot — the two models are selected per
    flow, not globally.
  src src/flow_exec.rs
  num Lines = 1,800

store flow_runs "Runs"
  is persisted run state — the reason a paused flow can be resumed at all
  why >
    Each run is a document under runs/ holding the current node, the variables
    object and the pause reason. Without it an approval node would be a hang
    rather than a pause. This is separate from the interval scheduler's
    in-memory history, which is deliberately not persisted.
  src src/flow_runs.rs

queue scheduled_tasks "Follow-ups" x1.2
  is persisted reminders the agent armed for itself, waiting for their moment
  why >
    When the agent calls schedule_followup it writes a task bound to the
    current session, with a reschedule depth so it cannot arm itself forever.
    Each daemon tick claims the due ones and replays them into their bound
    session — a workshop chat or a gateway conversation. This is what lets an
    agent say it will check back in an hour and actually do it.
  src src/scheduled_tasks.rs src/tools/schedule_followup.rs

job reminders_job "Calendar reminders"
  is >
    a separate ticker over pod-local events, only alive when the calendar pack
    is on
  why >
    Spawned as its own loop rather than folded into the flow poll, because
    event reminders need a finer cadence than a thirty-second flow tick and
    should not be delayed by a long-running flow. It reads the calendar app's
    own SQLite database directly, since both live in the same process.
  src src/apps/calendar/reminders.rs

group pod_apps "The pod apps"
  note an OS layer that grew inside the agent

svc app_sdk "App SDK" x1.4
  is >
    the OS layer: lends each app a database, a blob namespace, an event hub
    and an identity
  why >
    The ecosystem apps are moving off cloud Postgres and into the user's own
    pod, running inside this binary against the pod's disk. An app wires into
    exactly three existing seams — the router, the tool registry fallthrough,
    and the pack manifest — and never touches the turn loop. Because a managed
    pod has exactly one owner, no app ever resolves per-request identity; it
    trusts the process.
  src src/apps/mod.rs docs/POD_NATIVE_APPS_PLAN.md
  num Seams touched = 3
  num Built-in apps = 3

store sqlite_store "SQLite tier" x1.3
  is one database file per app on the pod's block volume, opened lazily
  why >
    Rows, relationships, indexes and full-text search — the hot structured
    tier. The pool is capped at a single connection and opened lazily, and the
    library is bundled so a container build needs no system sqlite. It
    requires a real POSIX filesystem because of WAL, which is the constraint
    that decided the pod needs a block volume rather than object storage
    alone.
  src src/apps/storage.rs
  uses sqlx sqlite
  num Pool size = 1

store blob_store "Blob tier"
  is opaque bytes under string keys — uploads, attachments, exports
  why >
    The trait is deliberately S3-shaped even though today's only
    implementation writes to the local filesystem, so a presigned S3 or R2
    backend can replace it without any app code changing. Large binaries go
    here and never into SQLite.
  src src/apps/blobs.rs

queue event_hub "Event hub"
  is a per-app broadcast channel driving websocket push to outside clients
  why >
    Pod-native apps are backend-only — any UI lives outside the pod — so this
    exists to tell those clients that something changed. One in-process
    broadcast ring per app, no Redis and no cross-process fan-out, because a
    managed pod serves exactly one user. A subscriber that lags past the ring
    resyncs over REST instead.
  src src/apps/events.rs
  uses tokio

svc notes_app "Notes"
  is flat markdown notes with colour-coded categories, addressed by slug
  why >
    No folders by design: one owner holds many notes, each with a slug,
    organised by at most twelve categories. It ships a portable export format
    and a sanitized markdown-to-HTML renderer for public share pages, so a
    shared note is served by the pod itself rather than by a cloud service.
  src src/apps/notes/
  uses comrak ammonia
  num Tools = 7

svc calendar_app "Calendar"
  is events, guests and invite responses, with its own timezone handling
  why >
    The most involved of the three: beyond CRUD it handles guest lists, invite
    responses pushed back through a webhook, and a reminder ticker of its own.
    Timezone logic is a dedicated module rather than scattered, because an
    event is the one thing where getting the offset wrong is immediately
    visible to a human.
  src src/apps/calendar/
  uses chrono-tz
  num Tools = 13

svc drive_app "Drive"
  is folders, files, starring and trash, with the bytes in the blob tier
  why >
    The newest of the three and the reason the blob tier exists at all —
    metadata goes to SQLite while contents go to the blob store, which is why
    the trait was shaped like S3 from the start. Share links register with the
    coordinator so a neutral public URL can route back to this specific pod.
  src src/apps/drive/
  num Tools = 10

ext coordinator "Coordinator"
  is a neutral relay that maps a share token to whichever pod owns it
  why >
    Every call to it is best-effort on purpose. The pod already serves its own
    share URL, so an unreachable or unconfigured coordinator only means the
    link is not on the neutral domain — sharing still works. That is the
    difference between a nicety and a dependency.
  src src/apps/coordinator.rs docs/COORDINATOR_PLAN.md

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# connections
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

cli_repl -call-> seeder "on startup"
  as e_cli_seed
  carry seed::ensure_defaults()
  why >
    Both binaries do this before anything else, so the data directory always
    exists and is populated. It is idempotent and cheap enough to run on every
    boot.
  vol 0.2

cli_repl -call-> agent_runtime "build the agent"
  as e_cli_runtime
  carry >
    build_agent_runtime(persona, cwd, model, approval_mode, RuntimeOptions)
  why >
    Called once at startup and again whenever a slash command changes persona,
    model or working directory. There is no partial rebuild — the system
    prompt and tool set are recomputed together.
  vol 0.4

cli_repl -call-> turn_runner "one REPL line"
  as e_cli_turn
  carry state.continue_with(input) → TurnRunner::run(state, step_guard)
  why >
    Each non-command line becomes a turn, and the resulting state is kept for
    the next one. The REPL reuses a single TurnRunner across the whole
    session, and prints a brief notice on turns where compaction fired.
  vol 0.9

daemon_bin -spawn-> workshop_api "hosts the API"
  as e_daemon_api
  carry tokio::spawn(workshop_api serve on --api-port, default 3002)
  why >
    Only when a workshop key is present. This is what lets one process be both
    the scheduler and the REST surface, which is the normal deployment.
  vol 0.3

daemon_bin -call-> poll_loop "start polling"
  as e_daemon_poll
  carry daemon::run(flows_dir, persona, model, poll_seconds, once)
  why >
    The main loop. With --once it makes a single pass and exits, which is how
    it is tested and how a cron-driven deployment uses it.
  vol 0.4

daemon_bin -spawn-> reminders_job "tick the calendar"
  as e_daemon_reminders
  carry tokio::spawn(apps::calendar::reminders::reminders_loop())
  why >
    Spawned unconditionally but inert unless the calendar pack is enabled.
    Keeping it off the flow tick means a slow flow cannot delay a reminder.
  vol 0.2

daemon_bin -spawn-> channels "self-heal"
  as e_daemon_heal
  carry tokio::spawn(metalcraft_gateway::heal_loop())
  why >
    Re-registers the pod's inbound webhook if the gateway has forgotten it, so
    a pod that was offline reconnects without a human. Best-effort and silent
    when there is nothing to fix.
  vol 0.15

workshop_api -call-> turn_runner "chat turn"
  as e_api_turn
  carry POST /api/v1/chats/{id}/turn → run_chat_turn
  why >
    The same turn primitive the REPL uses, with the reply sink pointed at an
    SSE stream instead of stdout. Tool events are emitted as they happen so
    the UI can show the agent working.
  vol 0.8

workshop_api -data-> gateway_ingress "webhook post"
  as e_api_ingress
  carry POST /webhook/gateway with an HMAC signature header
  why >
    The push half of inbound delivery. The signature is verified before the
    body is trusted, and the message is handed straight to the dedup check.
  vol 0.6

workshop_api -write-> persona_store "edit a persona"
  as e_api_persona
  carry GET|PUT|DELETE /api/v1/personas/{slug}
  why >
    Writes the same file on disk the agent reads at build time, so the change
    takes effect the next time the agent is built. There is no reload endpoint
    because none is needed.
  vol 0.4

workshop_api -call-> integration_packs "install & toggle"
  as e_api_packs
  carry >
    POST /api/v1/integration-packs/install · PUT
    /integration-packs/{id}/enabled
  why >
    Enabling a pack changes which tools resolve on the next agent build, and
    which pod apps mount. Disabling is the kill switch for a whole capability.
  vol 0.3

workshop_api -read-> flow_runs "inspect & resume"
  as e_api_runs
  carry GET /api/v1/flow-runs · POST /api/v1/flow-runs/{id}/resume
  why >
    How a human answers an approval node that paused days ago. The run
    document carries everything needed to continue mid-graph.
  vol 0.3

workshop_api -call-> app_sdk "mount routers"
  as e_api_apps
  carry apps::mount_app_routers(router) — nests each enabled app at /apps/<id>
  why >
    Apps mount outside the main auth layer and each re-checks the pod bearer
    itself, so an external client can talk to one app without being handed the
    whole API.
  vol 0.4

gateway_ingress -call-> turn_runner "inbound message"
  as e_ingress_turn
  carry route_gateway_inbound → run_chat_turn on the bound session
  why >
    Fires only after the dedup check passes. Everything downstream is
    identical to a workshop chat — the session preset is the only thing that
    differs, and it only decides where the reply goes.
  vol 0.7

gateway_ingress -read-> channels "long-poll pull"
  as e_ingress_channels
  carry GET {gateway}/api/v1/agent/inbound/next?wait=25
  why >
    The pull half of inbound delivery, for pods that cannot receive a webhook.
    Running both at once is safe because the dedup ledger is keyed on the
    gateway's message UUID.

persona_store -read-> agent_runtime "system prompt"
  as e_runtime_persona
  carry persona.build_system_prompt(skills_dir, cwd)
  why >
    Placeholders for cwd, available skills, sub-agent personas and installed
    packs are substituted into the template. Any the author did not use are
    appended as fallback sections, so a minimal persona still gets a usable
    prompt.

agent_runtime -call-> tool_registry "resolve tools"
  as e_runtime_registry
  carry >
    tools::create_registry_for_with_config(persona.resolved_tool_names(),
    ToolConfig)
  why >
    The persona's explicit tool list plus whatever its enabled packs
    contribute. Anything not on that list is never constructed, which is where
    least privilege actually happens.
  vol 0.6

agent_runtime -call-> react_graph "compile the graph"
  as e_runtime_graph
  carry >
    create_react_agent_with_options(model, registry, system_prompt,
    AgentOptions { .. })
  why >
    Produces a compiled graph stored behind an Arc so turns can share it
    cheaply. Rebuilding is the only way to change the prompt or tool set.
  vol 0.4

turn_runner -call-> compactor "compact first"
  as e_turn_compact
  carry context::compact_if_needed(state, CompactionConfig::default())
  why >
    Runs before every turn on every path. Putting it here rather than in each
    caller is what fixed one-shot runs silently skipping compaction.
  vol 0.8

turn_runner -call-> react_graph "run the executor"
  as e_turn_graph
  carry >
    Executor::new_from_arc(graph).max_steps(90).with_step_guard(g).run(state,
    "agent")
  why >
    Returns Completed, Interrupted or Failed — and all three preserve the
    conversation state, so a guard stop or a denied approval does not lose the
    turn. One shared constant sets the step ceiling for every caller.
  vol 1

react_graph -call-> llm_client "think"
  as e_graph_llm
  carry rig CompletionModel::completion(messages, tools)
  why >
    The model either answers or emits a tool call. Every request is
    snapshotted by the LLM-call hook when diagnostics are on.
  vol 1

react_graph -event-> approval_hook "before tool call"
  as e_graph_approval
  carry BeforeToolCallHook → Proceed | Deny(reason)
  why >
    Fires on every single tool call. A denial is fed back to the model as the
    tool result rather than aborting, so it can adapt and try something else.
  vol 0.9

approval_hook -call-> tool_registry "proceed"
  as e_approval_registry
  carry Tool::call(args) once the OperationKind clears
  why >
    Only reached for calls that were auto-approved or explicitly allowed. In
    interactive mode the prompt waits indefinitely rather than timing out into
    a denial.
  vol 0.9

react_graph -event-> step_guard "after each step"
  as e_graph_guard
  carry StepGuard → GuardAction::Continue | Stop
  why >
    Prints the call and its result as the agent runs, and decides whether the
    run has gone pathological. A Stop surfaces to the caller as an Interrupted
    outcome with the state intact.
  vol 0.9

step_guard -write-> diagnostics "turn snapshot"
  as e_guard_diag
  carry sessions/<id>/turn_NNN.json — the full message array
  why >
    Written after every step when diagnostics are enabled. Together with the
    config-change records it is enough to reconstruct exactly what the agent
    saw.

llm_client -write-> diagnostics "call snapshot"
  as e_llm_diag
  carry LlmCallHook → sessions/<id> + traces/<id>/otlp-trace.json
  why >
    Two writers on one hook: a bespoke JSON snapshot and an OpenTelemetry
    GenAI span. The trace is the one you can ship to a collector without a
    translator.

compactor -call-> llm_client "summarise"
  as e_compact_llm
  carry >
    a rig agent with a summarizer preamble over all but the last 10 messages
  why >
    A separate lighter model instance, so a long conversation is not
    summarized at the expensive model's price. The summary replaces the old
    messages as one assistant turn.
  vol 0.3

tool_registry -spawn-> core_tools "register built-ins"
  as e_registry_core
  carry match name { "read_file" => .., "bash" => .., "web_fetch" => .. }
  why >
    The hand-written Rust tools, matched by exact name. A persona that does
    not name bash simply never has it.
  vol 0.6

tool_registry -spawn-> meta_tools "register meta"
  as e_registry_meta
  carry persona_* skill_* flow_* pack_* key_* diagnostics_*
  why >
    The tools that let the agent rewrite its own configuration. Reads
    auto-approve and writes prompt, which is the whole safety story for
    self-modification.

tool_registry -spawn-> sub_agent_tool "register"
  as e_registry_sub
  carry sub_agent { task, tool_set: read_only | full | all, pack?, persona? }
  why >
    Needs the API key, model and system prompt at construction time, because
    it builds a whole child agent rather than making a call.
  vol 0.3

tool_registry -spawn-> native_integrations "register by pack"
  as e_registry_native
  carry native_pack_tool_names(pack_id) for each enabled pack
  why >
    Registered as a family rather than individually, so enabling the email
    pack brings every email_* tool at once and disabling it removes them all.
  vol 0.4

tool_registry -call-> app_sdk "claim the name"
  as e_registry_app
  carry >
    apps::try_register_app_tool(registry, unknown) -> Result<ToolRegistry,
    ToolRegistry>
  why >
    Third stage of the fallthrough. The return type is the registry either
    way, which is how an app takes ownership of a name without the registry
    knowing anything about apps.
  vol 0.4

tool_registry -spawn-> http_api_tool "last resort"
  as e_registry_http
  carry <data_dir>/api_tools/<name>.json
  why >
    Final stage: any name still unclaimed is looked up as a JSON tool
    definition on disk. This is why most pack tools need no Rust, and why an
    unknown tool is a missing file rather than a compile error.
  vol 0.6

http_api_tool -read-> key_store "expand $NAME"
  as e_http_keys
  carry $GITHUB_TOKEN in a header or URL, resolved when the request is built
  why >
    Late binding on purpose: a tool definition never contains a secret, so it
    is safe to read, share, or show the model. Rotating a key takes effect on
    the very next call.
  vol 0.6

sub_agent_tool -spawn-> react_graph "nested agent"
  as e_sub_graph
  carry a child registry + graph, 120 s timeout, max_steps(90)
  why >
    The child returns its final answer plus which tools it used and how many
    turns it took, so the parent can judge the cost. This is the machinery
    behind the Orchestrator persona, which delegates rather than doing the
    work itself.
  vol 0.3

core_tools -read-> skill_store "load a skill"
  as e_core_skill
  carry load_skill reads <skills_dir>/<skill>.md and strips the frontmatter
  why >
    The parameter schema restricts the skill argument to an enum of exactly
    the persona's allowed skills, so the model cannot request one it should
    not have. Loading on demand keeps sixteen methodologies out of the system
    prompt.
  vol 0.4

core_tools -write-> scheduled_tasks "arm a follow-up"
  as e_core_sched
  carry >
    schedule_followup { when, text } bound to session_binding +
    reschedule_depth
  why >
    The agent scheduling itself. The depth counter is what stops a follow-up
    from arming another follow-up forever.
  vol 0.3

core_tools -call-> channels "send a reply"
  as e_core_channels
  carry gateway_send_message → POST {url}/api/v1/messages/send
  why >
    In a tool-only session the agent has no stdout, so replying is itself a
    tool call routed through the session's reply sink. That is why say_to_user
    is a terminal tool.

seeder -write-> persona_store "write defaults"
  as e_seed_persona
  carry include_str! seeds → personas/*.json, version-gated
  why >
    Will not overwrite a persona you have edited, unless the bundled copy
    carries a newer version number. That exception is how fixes reach pods
    that are already running.
  vol 0.2

seeder -write-> skill_store "write defaults"
  as e_seed_skill
  carry 16 bundled markdown files → skills/
  why >
    Written once on first boot and then owned by the user. A fresh container
    is immediately useful with no network fetch.
  vol 0.2

seeder -write-> integration_packs "write defaults"
  as e_seed_packs
  carry 8 bundled packs → integration_packs/
  why >
    The bundled packs cover email and the Metalcraft ecosystem apps.
    Everything else is installed from the registry at runtime.
  vol 0.2

integration_packs -read-> registries "download a pack"
  as e_packs_registry
  carry GET packs.metalcraftai.com/api/v1/packs/{slug}/download
  why >
    Public and unauthenticated, so installing needs no credential. The
    download is size-capped before extraction, mirroring the cap applied at
    extract time.
  vol 0.2

integration_packs -write-> persona_store "unpack"
  as e_packs_persona
  carry install_from_zip writes personas/, skills/ and api_tools/ from the zip
  why >
    A pack installs into exactly the same directories a human edits by hand,
    so there is no separate managed namespace to reason about. Each install is
    also recorded in metalcraft.lock with a content hash.
  vol 0.3

integration_packs -read-> app_sdk "the on/off switch"
  as e_packs_sdk
  carry app.id() == pack.id — enable-state gates the app
  why >
    A deliberate identity: a pod app is enabled by enabling its pack, so there
    is one switch rather than two. Disabling the pack unmounts the routes and
    removes the tools.
  vol 0.3

meta_tools -write-> persona_store "persona_write"
  as e_meta_persona
  carry persona_write { slug, json } → personas/<slug>.json
  why >
    The agent editing its own scope. Classified as a meta-write, so it prompts
    in interactive mode rather than proceeding quietly.
  vol 0.3

meta_tools -write-> key_store "key_set"
  as e_meta_keys
  carry key_set { name, value } → the pod's key store
  why >
    Lets an agent finish configuring an integration it just installed. Listing
    returns masked values; revealing one is a separate explicit call.
  vol 0.3

poll_loop -read-> flow_store "who is due"
  as e_poll_flows
  carry load_enabled_flows(flows_dir) then is_due(last_started, schedule, tz)
  why >
    Every tick, from scratch — so editing a flow file takes effect within
    thirty seconds with no restart. A flow with an invalid cron expression is
    skipped rather than crashing the loop.
  vol 0.7

poll_loop -call-> flow_exec_v2 "run the flow"
  as e_poll_exec
  carry flow_exec::run_flow_v2(flow, variables)
  why >
    Marked running before it starts, so a long flow is not started twice by
    the next tick. Legacy v1 flows take a different path that just runs each
    reachable prompt node in traversal order.
  vol 0.6

poll_loop -read-> scheduled_tasks "anything due"
  as e_poll_sched
  carry run_due_scheduled_tasks — claim, then deliver
  why >
    Runs in the same tick as the flow poll. Claiming before running is what
    stops a slow follow-up from being fired twice.

flow_exec_v2 -write-> flow_runs "persist the run"
  as e_exec_runs
  carry <data>/runs/<run_id>.json — current node, variables, pause reason
  why >
    Written as the machine advances, which is the only reason an approval or
    wait node is a pause rather than a hang. A resume picks up mid-graph,
    possibly days later.
  vol 0.6

flow_exec_v2 -call-> turn_runner "prompt node"
  as e_exec_turn
  carry runtime::run_one_shot_task per prompt node
  why >
    A prompt node is a full agent turn with its own persona resolution — the
    node's persona, else the entry node's, else the daemon's flag. It gets
    compaction and the step guard like any other turn.
  vol 0.7

flow_exec_v2 -call-> tool_registry "tool node"
  as e_exec_tools
  carry a flow node invoking a registered tool directly
  why >
    No model in the loop for these — the flow author picked the tool and the
    arguments. This is how a flow does deterministic work between the parts
    that need judgement.
  vol 0.4

scheduled_tasks -call-> turn_runner "fire"
  as e_sched_turn
  carry the follow-up text replayed into its bound session
  why >
    Delivered back to whichever session armed it, so the reply lands in the
    same workshop chat or gateway conversation the user was already in.
  vol 0.4

reminders_job -read-> calendar_app "due events"
  as e_reminders_cal
  carry pod-local events whose reminder time has passed
  why >
    Reads the calendar app's SQLite database in-process, with no HTTP hop,
    because both live in the same binary. That co-location is the point of
    moving the apps into the pod.
  vol 0.3

app_sdk -spawn-> notes_app "lend syscalls"
  as e_sdk_notes
  carry >
    AppContext { SqliteStore, BlobStore, AppEventHub, OwnerIdentity, data_dir
    }
  why >
    An app receives its capabilities rather than reaching for them, so its
    private database and blob namespace are decided by the SDK. Nothing an app
    does can touch the turn loop.
  vol 0.4

app_sdk -spawn-> calendar_app "lend syscalls"
  as e_sdk_cal
  carry AppContext scoped to <data>/apps/metalcraft-calendar/
  why >
    Same context shape as every other app. The private scratch directory is
    the only filesystem an app is handed.
  vol 0.4

app_sdk -spawn-> drive_app "lend syscalls"
  as e_sdk_drive
  carry AppContext scoped to <data>/apps/metalcraft-drive/
  why >
    Drive is the app that actually exercises the blob tier, which is why the
    tier was designed before it shipped.
  vol 0.4

notes_app -write-> sqlite_store "rows + search"
  as e_notes_sql
  carry <data>/apps/metalcraft-notes/metalcraft-notes.db
  why >
    Note bodies, slugs, categories and the full-text index all live in one
    file. Migrations run on open, so an upgraded binary repairs its own
    schema.
  vol 0.6

calendar_app -write-> sqlite_store "events + guests"
  as e_cal_sql
  carry <data>/apps/metalcraft-calendar/metalcraft-calendar.db
  why >
    Events, guest lists and invite responses. The reminder ticker reads the
    same file from another task, which the single-connection pool serialises
    for free.

drive_app -write-> sqlite_store "metadata"
  as e_drive_sql
  carry folders, file records, starred and trash state
  why >
    Only the metadata — file contents never enter SQLite. That split is the
    rule the whole storage design is built around.
  vol 0.4

drive_app -write-> blob_store "file bytes"
  as e_drive_blob
  carry BlobStore::put(key, bytes) — LocalBlobStore today
  why >
    Deliberately S3-shaped so the local implementation can be swapped for a
    presigned object store without any app code changing. Uploads, attachments
    and exports all land here.

notes_app -event-> event_hub "note upserted"
  as e_notes_events
  carry tokio broadcast → /ws push
  why >
    Lets a note edited in one client appear live in another, even though the
    pod itself serves no UI. A subscriber that falls behind the ring resyncs
    over REST instead of receiving a partial history.

drive_app -call-> coordinator "register a share"
  as e_drive_coord
  carry { token → this pod, ref } so {coordinator}/p/{token} routes here
  why >
    Fire and forget. If the coordinator is unreachable the share still works
    on the pod's own public URL — it just is not on the neutral domain.
  vol 0.2

# ----------------------------------------------------------------------
# narrative
# ----------------------------------------------------------------------

tab what "What it does"

  h The Pod

  p >
    This is a self-hosted AI agent that runs on a box you own. One Rust
    binary, one required environment variable, and no cloud service in the
    path of a turn. You can drive it as a terminal REPL, as a one-shot
    command, or as a long-lived daemon that answers HTTP, replies to inbound
    messages, and fires scheduled workflows.

  p >
    The organising idea is the {{persona}}. A request does not run as "the
    agent" with everything available — it runs as [[a named, scoped tool
    set|persona_store]] with its own system prompt. The research persona is
    read-only. The devops persona can touch its own infrastructure and nothing
    else. The default Orchestrator mostly [[delegates|sub_agent_tool]],
    handing each sub-task to the specialist built for it.

  h What a turn actually is

  p >
    There are four ways in — the terminal, the scheduler, the REST API, and an
    inbound message from a messaging gateway — and they all converge on [[one
    function|turn_runner]]. It compacts the conversation if it has grown too
    long, then runs a ReAct loop: think, call a tool, observe, repeat, up to
    ninety steps.

  p >
    That convergence is not incidental. The comment in the source says it
    plainly: three separate turn paths meant a behaviour present in one could
    go silently missing from the others. Compaction used to be skipped on
    one-shot runs for exactly that reason.

  note >
    Hover any block for what it does and which files back it. Hover a
    connector for the concrete thing that crosses it — a function signature, a
    route, a file path. Highlighted phrases here are live.

  h The two safety rails

  p >
    Every tool call passes through [[an approval hook|approval_hook]] that
    classifies it and decides whether to proceed or ask. Writing a new file is
    automatic; overwriting an existing one asks. An unrecognised tool is
    treated as a shell command, so it asks — it fails safe rather than open.

  p >
    After every step, [[a guard|step_guard]] watches for the two ways an agent
    loop goes wrong: three consecutive turns where every tool errored, or the
    same call repeated byte-for-byte five times in a row. Tools that are
    genuinely polls get a much larger budget, so waiting on an async job is
    not mistaken for a runaway.

  h Everything else is a file

  p >
    Personas, skills, flows, HTTP tool definitions, packs and keys are all
    files in a data directory. [[The REST API|workshop_api]] reads and writes
    those same files, which is why an edit made from a desktop app takes
    effect on the next agent build with no reload step and no cache to
    invalidate.

tab how "How it's built"

  h Rust, and three crates it did not write

  p >
    The agent loop itself is [[a dependency|react_graph]], not code in this
    repo. The metalcraft framework supplies the graph, the executor, the tool
    trait and the hook system; rig supplies the model client; metalcraft-flows
    supplies the workflow data model. What this repo adds is the persona
    layer, the tools, the approval policy, the flow runtime and the API.

  p >
    That is a deliberate line. Because the loop is borrowed, all the
    interesting logic lands in hooks — and hooks are small, testable, and easy
    to reason about individually.

  h Adding a tool without writing Rust

  p >
    [[The registry|tool_registry]] matches a persona's tool names against the
    built-ins, then falls through: native pack tools, then pod apps, then a
    JSON file in api_tools/. That last stage is [[one generic HTTP
    tool|http_api_tool]] that reads a definition — method, URL, headers,
    parameter schema, body mapping — and becomes any REST call.

  p >
    GitHub, Linear, Cloudflare and Sentry integrations are therefore JSON
    documents, not code. Credentials never appear in them: a header reads
    $GITHUB_TOKEN and [[the key store|key_store]] resolves it when the request
    is built, which means a tool definition is safe to read, share, or hand to
    the model.

  p >
    The three integrations that did need real Rust are the ones that could not
    be a plain REST call — S3 request signing, an IMAP session with MIME
    parsing, and Twilio's signature scheme.

  h Flows are a state machine, not a script

  p >
    [[The flow executor|flow_exec_v2]] walks a graph one node at a time,
    threading a shared variables object and routing by output handle. Approval
    and wait nodes [[park the run to disk|flow_runs]] and a later resume picks
    it up mid-graph, possibly days afterwards. Without that persistence an
    approval node would be a hang rather than a pause.

  p >
    The scheduler reloads the flow directory from scratch every tick, so
    editing a flow file takes effect within thirty seconds and a bad cron
    expression skips one flow instead of crashing the loop. One detail catches
    people out: cron uses the six-field form with seconds, evaluated in the
    daemon's local timezone.

  code |
    cargo run --bin metalcraft-daemon -- --once --auto-approve

  h An operating system grew inside it

  p >
    The newest and least expected part of this codebase is [[the app
    SDK|app_sdk]]. The ecosystem apps — notes, calendar, drive — are moving
    off cloud Postgres and into the user's own pod, running inside this same
    binary against the pod's disk.

  p >
    Each app is lent a set of syscalls: [[a private SQLite
    database|sqlite_store]] for structured state, [[a blob
    namespace|blob_store]] for bytes, [[a broadcast hub|event_hub]] for
    pushing changes to clients outside the pod, and an owner identity. It
    wires into exactly three existing seams — the router, the registry
    fallthrough, and the pack manifest — and never touches the turn loop.

  p >
    Two consequences fall straight out of "a pod is one user". No app resolves
    per-request identity; it trusts the process. And [[the event
    hub|event_hub]] is a single in-process broadcast channel — no Redis, no
    cross-process fan-out, no per-user keying.

  ---

  p >
    The largest file here is [[the REST API|workshop_api]] at 4,344 lines and
    59 routes — about one line in seven of the whole codebase. That is worth
    knowing before you go looking for anything.

term "persona" >
  A JSON file defining an agent's name, system prompt, allowed tool names
  and skills. A request runs as a persona, so the tool list is the security
  boundary — anything not named is never constructed.
